<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Domingo Martínez de Irala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Domingo Martínez de Irala was a Basque conquistador who came to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a> and became the dominant figure of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. Elected governor by the settlers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, he deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvar-nunez-cabeza-de-vaca">Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca</a> in 1544 and ruled the colony for most of the period until his death, founding it on Guaraní alliances, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a> and mixed marriages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/domingo-martinez-de-irala.jpg" alt="Domingo Martínez de Irala" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domingo_Mart%C3%ADnez_de_Irala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Irala sailed for America in 1535 as a member of the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a> and took part in the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. He explored the Paraná and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> rivers with Juan de Ayolas, and he was commanding the rear-guard when Ayolas's advance party was wiped out by the Payaguá. Charles V had granted the colony a right, unique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>, to elect its own commander in such a case, and in August 1538 the conquistadors elected Irala Captain General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>.</p><p>In 1539 he began to move the settlers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, and the older town had been abandoned by 1541. He outlasted the royal appointee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvar-nunez-cabeza-de-vaca">Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca</a>, whom he sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to be tried as a traitor. Juan de Sanabria and his son Diego were named governor in 1547 and 1549 but never took up their commissions, and in 1552 the king confirmed Irala as governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Between 1537 and 1541 the conquistadors received women within the cuñadazgo, a Guaraní institution by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a> sealed alliances by giving women to newcomers, who thereby became brothers-in-law or sons-in-law. After 1541, with the settlers concentrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, this system gave way to the rancheadas: mass and violent seizures of Guaraní women, who were bought and sold, used as currency and shipped to the Portuguese port of São Vicente.</p><p>A 1545 letter by Pero Hernández records that Irala sold a free Cario woman to Tristán de Vallartas for a scarlet cloak and a velvet jacket. In his testament of 1556 Irala acknowledged nine children by seven Guaraní women, whom he called his criadas, or servants. Modern historians describe the mestizaje of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> as fundamentally a forced sexual relationship.</p><p>In 1555 Irala divided land among about 320 colonists as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>, which bound between 17,000 and 20,000 indigenous people; the first encomiendas provoked uprisings. He governed forcefully until his death in 1556, raising churches and public buildings, founding towns and subjugating the native population. Gonzalo de Mendoza succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Domingo Martínez de Irala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From Asunción, governors such as <b>Domingo Martínez de Irala</b> and Álvar Núñez Cabeza de Vaca ruled a frontier colony built on alliances with the Guaraní, many of whom were bound in encomiendas, and on mixed marriages that produced a large mestizo population.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Governorate of the Río de la Plata</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bergara, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.12,-2.41&amp;year=1539" aria-label="Bergara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Domingo Martínez de Irala"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4849 3886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4849 3886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.12,-2.41&amp;year=1539">What was here in 1539? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
